        "title": "Introduction",
        "key_prerequisites": [],
        "has_content_in_snapshot": true,
        "sections": [
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>Consider the function <span class=\"math\">[MATH: f(x) = x^2.]</span> The <strong>definite integral</strong> <strong>of</strong> <span class=\"math\">[MATH: \\mathbf f(x)]</span> <strong>between</strong> <span class=\"math\">[MATH: \\mathbf{x=1}]</span> and <span class=\"math\">[MATH: \\mathbf{x=2}]</span> is denoted</p>",
            "readable_text": "Consider the function [MATH: f(x) = x^2.] The definite integral of [MATH: \\mathbf f(x)] between [MATH: \\mathbf{x=1}] and [MATH: \\mathbf{x=2}] is denoted",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p><span class=\"math\">[MATH: \\int_1^2 x^2 \\, \\textrm d x.]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "[MATH: \\int_1^2 x^2 \\, \\textrm d x.]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>Geometrically, this definite integral can be interpreted as the <strong>signed area</strong> bounded between the curve <span class=\"math\">[MATH: y=f(x),]</span> the lines <span class=\"math\">[MATH: x=1]</span> and <span class=\"math\">[MATH: x=2,]</span> and the <span class=\"math\">[MATH: x]</span>-axis, as shown below.</p>",
            "readable_text": "Geometrically, this definite integral can be interpreted as the signed area bounded between the curve [MATH: y=f(x),] the lines [MATH: x=1] and [MATH: x=2,] and the [MATH: x]-axis, as shown below.",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "graphic",
            "normalized_html": "<p><img src=\"/graphics/2f9e5bf8afd908ee301b07893cfa7f12\"></p>",
            "readable_text": "[IMG: /graphics/2f9e5bf8afd908ee301b07893cfa7f12]",
            "images": [
              {
                "src": "/graphics/2f9e5bf8afd908ee301b07893cfa7f12",
                "alt": ""
              }
            ],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>The area is taken as positive (with a plus sign) if the corresponding region lies above the <span class=\"math\">[MATH: x]</span>-axis and as negative (with a minus sign) if the region lies below the <span class=\"math\">[MATH: x]</span>-axis. </p>",
            "readable_text": "The area is taken as positive (with a plus sign) if the corresponding region lies above the [MATH: x]-axis and as negative (with a minus sign) if the region lies below the [MATH: x]-axis.",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>To calculate this area, we use a theorem known as the <strong>fundamental theorem of calculus</strong>, which states the following:</p>",
            "readable_text": "To calculate this area, we use a theorem known as the fundamental theorem of calculus, which states the following:",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<blockquote><p><em>If <span class=\"math\">[MATH: f(x)]</span> is a function that&#x27;s continuous on an interval <span class=\"math\">[MATH: [a,b]]</span>, and there exists a function <span class=\"math\">[MATH: F(x)]</span> such that <span class=\"math\">[MATH: F&#x27;(x) = f(x)]</span> on <span class=\"math\">[MATH: [a,b]]</span>, then</em></p></blockquote>",
            "readable_text": "> If [MATH: f(x)] is a function that's continuous on an interval [MATH: [a,b]], and there exists a function [MATH: F(x)] such that [MATH: F'(x) = f(x)] on [MATH: [a,b]], then",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p><span class=\"math\">[MATH: \\int_a^b f(x)\\,\\textrm{d}x = F(b) - F(a).]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "[MATH: \\int_a^b f(x)\\,\\textrm{d}x = F(b) - F(a).]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>So how do we use this to calculate our definite integral <span class=\"math\">[MATH: \\displaystyle \\int_1^2 x^2\\,\\textrm{d}x?]</span> We just need to follow a few steps:</p>",
            "readable_text": "So how do we use this to calculate our definite integral [MATH: \\displaystyle \\int_1^2 x^2\\,\\textrm{d}x?] We just need to follow a few steps:",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p><strong> Step 1</strong>: Find the <em>indefinite</em> integral (also known as the antiderivative) of the function using the power rule. We can ignore the constant of integration.\n<span class=\"math\">[MATH: \\int x^2 \\, \\textrm d x = \\dfrac{x^3}{3}]</span> </p>",
            "readable_text": "Step 1: Find the indefinite integral (also known as the antiderivative) of the function using the power rule. We can ignore the constant of integration.\n[MATH: \\int x^2 \\, \\textrm d x = \\dfrac{x^3}{3}]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p><strong>Step 2</strong>: Write the lower and upper limit next to the result.\n<span class=\"math\">[MATH: \\int_1^2 x^2 \\, \\textrm d x = \\dfrac{x^3}{3}\\Bigg|_{\\color{red}1}^{\\color{blue}2}]</span> </p>",
            "readable_text": "Step 2: Write the lower and upper limit next to the result.\n[MATH: \\int_1^2 x^2 \\, \\textrm d x = \\dfrac{x^3}{3}\\Bigg|_{\\color{red}1}^{\\color{blue}2}]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p><strong>Step 3</strong>: Evaluate the antiderivative between the upper and lower limit, and subtract them.</p>",
            "readable_text": "Step 3: Evaluate the antiderivative between the upper and lower limit, and subtract them.",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p><span class=\"math\">[MATH: \u222b_{1}^{2}x^{2}dx|= ((2)^{3})/(3) \u2212 ((1)^{3})/(3); = (8)/(3) \u2212 (1)/(3); = (7)/(3).]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "[MATH: \u222b_{1}^{2}x^{2}dx|= ((2)^{3})/(3) \u2212 ((1)^{3})/(3); = (8)/(3) \u2212 (1)/(3); = (7)/(3).]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>Therefore, we conclude that\n<span class=\"math\">[MATH: \\int_1^2 f(x)\\,\\textrm{d}x = \\dfrac73.]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "Therefore, we conclude that\n[MATH: \\int_1^2 f(x)\\,\\textrm{d}x = \\dfrac73.]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          }
        ],
        "images": [
          {
            "src": "/graphics/2f9e5bf8afd908ee301b07893cfa7f12",
            "alt": ""
          }
        ],
        "free_entry_blanks": [],
        "select_lists": [],
        "readable_text": "Content:\nConsider the function [MATH: f(x) = x^2.] The definite integral of [MATH: \\mathbf f(x)] between [MATH: \\mathbf{x=1}] and [MATH: \\mathbf{x=2}] is denoted\n\nContent:\n[MATH: \\int_1^2 x^2 \\, \\textrm d x.]\n\nContent:\nGeometrically, this definite integral can be interpreted as the signed area bounded between the curve [MATH: y=f(x),] the lines [MATH: x=1] and [MATH: x=2,] and the [MATH: x]-axis, as shown below.\n\nGraphic:\n[IMG: /graphics/2f9e5bf8afd908ee301b07893cfa7f12]\n\nContent:\nThe area is taken as positive (with a plus sign) if the corresponding region lies above the [MATH: x]-axis and as negative (with a minus sign) if the region lies below the [MATH: x]-axis.\n\nContent:\nTo calculate this area, we use a theorem known as the fundamental theorem of calculus, which states the following:\n\nContent:\n> If [MATH: f(x)] is a function that's continuous on an interval [MATH: [a,b]], and there exists a function [MATH: F(x)] such that [MATH: F'(x) = f(x)] on [MATH: [a,b]], then\n\nContent:\n[MATH: \\int_a^b f(x)\\,\\textrm{d}x = F(b) - F(a).]\n\nContent:\nSo how do we use this to calculate our definite integral [MATH: \\displaystyle \\int_1^2 x^2\\,\\textrm{d}x?] We just need to follow a few steps:\n\nContent:\nStep 1: Find the indefinite integral (also known as the antiderivative) of the function using the power rule. We can ignore the constant of integration.\n[MATH: \\int x^2 \\, \\textrm d x = \\dfrac{x^3}{3}]\n\nContent:\nStep 2: Write the lower and upper limit next to the result.\n[MATH: \\int_1^2 x^2 \\, \\textrm d x = \\dfrac{x^3}{3}\\Bigg|_{\\color{red}1}^{\\color{blue}2}]\n\nContent:\nStep 3: Evaluate the antiderivative between the upper and lower limit, and subtract them.\n\nContent:\n[MATH: \u222b_{1}^{2}x^{2}dx|= ((2)^{3})/(3) \u2212 ((1)^{3})/(3); = (8)/(3) \u2212 (1)/(3); = (7)/(3).]\n\nContent:\nTherefore, we conclude that\n[MATH: \\int_1^2 f(x)\\,\\textrm{d}x = \\dfrac73.]"
      },
