    "readable_text": "Item 1 \u2014 Tutorial: Introduction\n\nContent:\nConsider the function [MATH: f(x) = x^2.] The definite integral of [MATH: \\mathbf f(x)] between [MATH: \\mathbf{x=1}] and [MATH: \\mathbf{x=2}] is denoted\n\nContent:\n[MATH: \\int_1^2 x^2 \\, \\textrm d x.]\n\nContent:\nGeometrically, this definite integral can be interpreted as the signed area bounded between the curve [MATH: y=f(x),] the lines [MATH: x=1] and [MATH: x=2,] and the [MATH: x]-axis, as shown below.\n\nGraphic:\n[IMG: /graphics/2f9e5bf8afd908ee301b07893cfa7f12]\n\nContent:\nThe area is taken as positive (with a plus sign) if the corresponding region lies above the [MATH: x]-axis and as negative (with a minus sign) if the region lies below the [MATH: x]-axis.\n\nContent:\nTo calculate this area, we use a theorem known as the fundamental theorem of calculus, which states the following:\n\nContent:\n> If [MATH: f(x)] is a function that's continuous on an interval [MATH: [a,b]], and there exists a function [MATH: F(x)] such that [MATH: F'(x) = f(x)] on [MATH: [a,b]], then\n\nContent:\n[MATH: \\int_a^b f(x)\\,\\textrm{d}x = F(b) - F(a).]\n\nContent:\nSo how do we use this to calculate our definite integral [MATH: \\displaystyle \\int_1^2 x^2\\,\\textrm{d}x?] We just need to follow a few steps:\n\nContent:\nStep 1: Find the indefinite integral (also known as the antiderivative) of the function using the power rule. We can ignore the constant of integration.\n[MATH: \\int x^2 \\, \\textrm d x = \\dfrac{x^3}{3}]\n\nContent:\nStep 2: Write the lower and upper limit next to the result.\n[MATH: \\int_1^2 x^2 \\, \\textrm d x = \\dfrac{x^3}{3}\\Bigg|_{\\color{red}1}^{\\color{blue}2}]\n\nContent:\nStep 3: Evaluate the antiderivative between the upper and lower limit, and subtract them.\n\nContent:\n[MATH: \u222b_{1}^{2}x^{2}dx|= ((2)^{3})/(3) \u2212 ((1)^{3})/(3); = (8)/(3) \u2212 (1)/(3); = (7)/(3).]\n\nContent:\nTherefore, we conclude that\n[MATH: \\int_1^2 f(x)\\,\\textrm{d}x = \\dfrac73.]\n\n---\n\nItem 2 \u2014 Example: Evaluating the Definite Integral of a Polynomial Function\n\nExample Prompt:\nEvaluate [MATH: \\displaystyle\\int_0^4 x \\, \\textrm d x.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nTaking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n[MATH: \u222b_{0}^{4}xdx|= (x^{1 + 1})/(1 + 1)|_{0}^{4}; = (x^{2})/(2)|_{0}^{4}; = (4^{2})/(2) \u2212 (0^{2})/(2); = (16)/(2); = 8.]\n\n---\n\nItem 3 \u2014 Question 1\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: \u222b_{\u2212 2}^{2}x^{3}dx =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 16]\nb. [MATH: (1)/(4)]\nc. [MATH: (1)/(16)]\nd. [MATH: 4]\ne. [MATH: 0]\n\n---\n\nItem 4 \u2014 Question 2\n\nPrompt:\nEvaluate [MATH: \u222b_{\u2212 5}^{4}x^{2}dx.]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (125)/(3)]\nb. [MATH: 189]\nc. [MATH: (189)/(2)]\nd. [MATH: (64)/(3)]\ne. [MATH: 63]\n\n---\n\nItem 5 \u2014 Example: Evaluating the Definite Integral of a Rational Function\n\nExample Prompt:\nEvaluate [MATH: \\displaystyle \\int _{1}^2\\dfrac 1 {x^3}\\, \\textrm d x.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nTaking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n[MATH: \u222b_{1}^{2}(1)/(x^{3})dx|= \u222b_{1}^{2}x^{\u2212 3}dx; = (x^{\u2212 3 + 1})/(\u2212 3 + 1)|_{1}^{2}; = \u2212 (x^{\u2212 2})/(2)|_{1}^{2}; = \u2212 (1)/(2)(2^{\u2212 2} \u2212 1^{\u2212 2}); = \u2212 (1)/(2)((1)/(4) \u2212 1); = \u2212 (1)/(2)(\u2212 (3)/(4)); = (3)/(8).]\n\n---\n\nItem 6 \u2014 Question 3\n\nPrompt:\nEvaluate [MATH: \u222b_{1/2}^{1}(1)/(x^{6})dx.]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: \u2212 (63)/(5)]\nb. [MATH: (31)/(5)]\nc. [MATH: \u2212 (7)/(5)]\nd. [MATH: \u2212 (31)/(5)]\ne. [MATH: (63)/(5)]\n\n---\n\nItem 7 \u2014 Question 4\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: \u222b_{1}^{2}(1)/(x^{4})dx =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (7)/(24)]\nb. [MATH: \u2212 (7)/(12)]\nc. [MATH: \u2212 (7)/(24)]\nd. [MATH: (7)/(12)]\ne. [MATH: (31)/(96)]\n\n---\n\nItem 8 \u2014 Example: Evaluating the Definite Integral of a Radical Function\n\nExample Prompt:\nCalculate [MATH: \\displaystyle\\int_1^4\\sqrt x\\, \\textrm d x.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nTaking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n[MATH: \u222b_{1}^{4}sqrt(x)dx|= \u222b_{1}^{4}x^{1/2}dx; = (2)/(3)x^{3/2}|_{1}^{4}; = (2)/(3)(4^{3/2} \u2212 1^{3/2}); = (2)/(3)(sqrt(64) \u2212 1); = (2)/(3)(8 \u2212 1); = (14)/(3).]\n\n---\n\nItem 9 \u2014 Question 5\n\nPrompt:\nEvaluate [MATH: \u222b_{0}^{4}sqrt(x^{5})dx.]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 448]\nb. [MATH: (256)/(7)]\nc. [MATH: 128]\nd. [MATH: (7)/(256)]\ne. [MATH: (128)/(7)]\n\n---\n\nItem 10 \u2014 Question 6\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: \u222b_{0}^{1}root(x^{3}, 5)dx =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (5)/(8)]\nb. [MATH: (5)/(4)]\nc. [MATH: 0]\nd. [MATH: \u2212 (5)/(4)]\ne. [MATH: \u2212 (5)/(3)]"
  }
